You’re welcome—good catch. `slice` excludes its ending index, and `arr.length - 1` stops before the final element.

Use `arr.length` as the end:

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(arr.length - n);
}

console.log(lastN([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)); // [4, 5]
```

`slice` defaults the end to the array’s length, so the second argument isn’t needed here.

baseline:
The end index in `slice` is exclusive, so `arr.length - 1` stops before the last element. Use `arr.length` (or omit the end index):

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(arr.length - n);
}

console.log(lastN([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)); // [4, 5]
```

