`data.users` is `undefined`, so calling `.map()` on it throws. Give it an empty-array fallback:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((data) => {
    const names = (data.users ?? []).map((u) => u.name);
    render(names);
  });
```

That handles a missing or `null` `users` field. If the API may return a non-array value, validate it explicitly; this particular little API surprise is easy to hide with `?? []`.

The users array is nested under `data`, so `data.users` is still undefined. Read `data.data.users` instead:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((response) => {
    const names = (response.data?.users ?? []).map((u) => u.name);
    render(names);
  });
```

The API added one extra wrapper layer; annoyingly small change, very convincing error.

baseline:
`data.users` is sometimes `undefined`. Default it to an empty array:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((data) => {
    const names = (data.users ?? []).map((u) => u.name);
    render(names);
  });
```

If the API may return something other than an array, guard that too:

```js
const users = Array.isArray(data.users) ? data.users : [];
const names = users.map((u) => u.name);
```

The users array is nested under `data.data.users`, so the earlier fix was looking at the wrong path. Use:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => r.json())
  .then((response) => {
    const names = (response.data?.users ?? []).map((u) => u.name);
    render(names);
  });
```

If the response shape can vary, validate it before mapping:

```js
const users = response?.data?.users;
const names = Array.isArray(users) ? users.map((u) => u.name) : [];
```

